        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The harmful portion of sunlight, the ultraviolet radiation at short wavelengths (<290 nanometers), is absorbed by Earth's atmosphere, especially by the stratospheric ozone layer. Space technology provides the opportunity for in situ investigations on the responses of terrestrial organisms to the full spectrum of extraterrestrial solar UV radiation. During the Spacelab Mission D-2, spores of different strains of Bacillus subtilis, cells of the radiation resistant bacterium Deinococcus radiodurans, and plasmid DNA of Escherichia coli were exposed to space vacuum and/or selected wavelengths and intensities of extraterrestrial solar UV radiation. <p>
<p>
APPROACH:<BR>
The investigation studied the effects caused by the space environment by determining the changes in the genetic material of the biological samples. The studied parameters included cell inactivation, induction of mutations, efficiency of repair, and chemical changes of DNA. The B. subtilis and D. radiodurans samples were prepared by transferring 20 microliters of bacterial suspension onto quartz discs of 7 mm diameter and dried overnight. The biofilm samples included immobilized dried spores of B. subtilis.
<p>
All samples were accommodated in two exposure trays mounted on the User Support Structure (USS) in the Shuttle's payload bay with free access to space. Each exposure tray consisted of four compartments, two vented and two hermetically sealed. Each compartment had an upper layer (samples were exposed to sunlight) and a bottom layer as "dark" controls (no exposure to sunlight). Six compartments housed biological samples (total number of biological samples was 308); the other two compartments contained biofilm samples.
<p>
The samples in the vented compartments were exposed to the space vacuum during the entire D-2 mission. On Mission Day 8, the Shuttle flew two orbits in a "bay sun attitude", pointing its payload bay towards the sun. During these two orbits, the upper layers of the compartments were exposed to extraterrestrial sunlight. The exposure time was controlled by a shutter system, which opened to allowed precise irradiation intervals for the different compartments between 5 and 120 minutes. An optical filter system allowed the passage of certain wavelength ranges of UV radiation. <p>

Each exposure tray was equipped with a recording system for temperature and ionizing radiation. For most of the D-2 mission, the temperature in the compartments ranged between -30 and +16 degrees Celsius. During the "bay sun phase" the temperature increased to + 36 degrees Celsius. <p>

An additional tray with identical samples was used as the ground control and was subjected to simulated flight parameters (vacuum and temperature profile) to match the conditions of the space flight experiment.
<p>
RESULTS: <BR>
(1) Effects of space vacuum: Samples that were in the vented compartments and kept in the dark during the mission (bottom layer) were used to study the responses to space vacuum. B. subtilis spores were less affected by the vacuum treatment than the other tested organisms. About 70% of all B. subtilis spores of the wild strain were recovered viable after the mission, when shielded against solar UV radiation (regardless if exposed to space vacuum or kept in air at atmospheric pressure). In comparison, the survival rate of the repair deficient strains of B. subtilis responded more sensitively, and the survival rate of vacuum exposed spores was reduced by a factor of 2 compared to samples kept at atmospheric pressure. This result suggests that vacuum treatment leads to injury of the spore DNA, probably to breakage of DNA strands. <p>
<p>
The cells of D. radioduran showed a much lower survival rate - about 2 percent. This is probably a result of the long storage time in air before and after flight (about 5 months before flight and 1 months afterwards). Another indication that DNA is the critical target for vacuum treatment was the higher mutagenesis rate to histidine prototrophy in B. subtilis spores. Compared to the samples kept in the sealed compartments, the mutation rate in the spores exposed to space vacuum was significantly elevated. The determining factor differed between the four tested B. subtilis strains and ranged from factors of 1.3 to 3.1; for the wild strain a factor of 2 was measured. <p>
<p>
(2) Responses to solar UV radiation: In order to evaluate the impact of the extraterrestrial spectrum of solar UV radiation on biological systems, action spectroscopy was utilized. During the "bay sun period" on flight Day 8, the samples were exposed to narrow bands of solar UV radiation in the range of 190 to 290 nm. Effect curves for inactivation of B. subtilis spores at different wavelengths were obtained. When B. subtilis spores were exposed to space vacuum during the irradiation period, the survival curves were lowered compared to the samples kept under atmospheric conditions. The combined action of solar UV light and vacuum lead to UV hypersensitivity by a factor of 2.5 to 4.5 compared to the response to solar UV alone.  This result demonstrates the importance of the ozone layer in protecting life on Earth, which absorbs the shorter wavelength radiation.<end>
